 "interpretation": [
  {
   "heading": "1. The pickaxe disappeared",
   "body": "Old record: pickaxe 30%, sword 10%, table 90%. Control with the recipe and death fixes: pickaxe 0%, sword 60%, table 83%. Both crafts need the same thing: one wood, standing next to a table. The agent reaches that state in most episodes, and it now consistently presses one crafting key and never the other. The potential weights the pickaxe three times the sword (3.0 vs 1.0), so the shaping pushes the other way. Across all seven runs only three ever made a pickaxe (vanilla 24%, map 7%, old record 30%) and only two ever made a sword."
  },
  {
   "heading": "2. The map model's gain is mostly sleep",
   "body": "WAKE_UP fires in 83-98% of episodes for every map run that learned to sleep, and 0% for vanilla. Remove it and the fixed map model (4.20) is below vanilla (4.44); map + potential (4.97) and the control (5.03) stay above it. So the evidence that the map itself helps is weak; the potential is what adds tech progress. Why map runs learn to sleep is an open question. It is systematic (every map run except the hand-written rewards one does it), not seed noise."
  },
  {
   "heading": "3. It is not the measurement",
   "body": "Every run was scored on the same fixed worlds, and re-scoring two old checkpoints with today's code reproduced them to the last digit. The pickaxe gap (9/30 vs 0/30) is far larger than evaluation noise."
  },
  {
   "heading": "4. Three explanations for the pickaxe, and the test for each",
   "body": "(a) Run-to-run variance. One training seed per run, and one run moves 3.60-4.68 between its own checkpoints. Two trainings of identical code can settle into different habits. Test: a second seed of the control configuration.\n(b) Valid-action suppression (Zabounidis et al. 2026, verified). Crafting keys do nothing in most states, so the gradient pushes them down there, and shared weights carry that into the table states where they work. Which key survives may be close to arbitrary per run, which fits (a) too. Test: experiment A, the probability of each crafting key in table-with-wood states for both checkpoints; no training needed.\n(c) The fixes changed the incentive. Before the death fix, dying kept the potential, including the pickaxe's capability term (2.0 per tier held), so a pickaxe held at death was a permanent bonus. The old 30% may have been partly propped up by that bug. Test: honest_map carries the same fixes; if it also skips the pickaxe, the fixes are implicated, and a run with only the death fix would isolate it."
  },
  {
   "heading": "5. What to run, cheapest first",
   "body": "Experiment A on the control and the old record (hours, no training). Score honest_map when it finishes. Then second seeds of the control and the old record, since single runs cannot separate (a) from (c), and reviewers expect 5+ seeds anyway. Explain the WAKE_UP gap before claiming the map model helps."
  }
 ],
